<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Nausea: Best Points" description="Learn how to use acupressure for nausea relief. Step-by-step guide to PC6, ST36, and LI4 points for pregnancy, chemotherapy, and motion sickness nausea." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Nausea", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-nausea/" }]} datePublished="2025-04-15" dateModified="2026-06-01" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Nausea: Best Points for Fast Relief</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Nausea is among the most uncomfortable sensations the human body can produce. Whether from motion sickness, pregnancy, chemotherapy, or post-operative recovery, the urge to vomit can be debilitating. Acupressure for nausea offers a drug-free, easy-to-do option that many people find genuinely helpful — and one point, PC6 (Neiguan) on the inner wrist, has more research support behind it than any other point on this site.</p><div class="mb-8 p-4 bg-forest-50 dark:bg-forest-900/20 border-l-4 border-forest-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><strong>Research Summary:</strong> The strongest evidence on this site concerns PC6 (Neiguan) for post-operative nausea and vomiting. A long-running Cochrane systematic review (Lee A, Fan LTY, Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2009; CD003281, <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19370583/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300 dark:hover:text-forest-200">PMID 19370583</a>) found that non-invasive P6/PC6 stimulation reduces post-operative nausea and vomiting. The evidence for PC6 in chemotherapy-related and pregnancy-related nausea is generally positive but carries more uncertainty.</p></div><section id="understanding" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Understanding Nausea</h2><p class="mb-3">Nausea is not a disease but a symptom with many possible triggers. It involves the brainstem's vomiting center, which can be activated by the inner ear (motion), the gut (food poisoning, pregnancy), or higher brain centers (stress, anxiety, chemotherapy).</p><p class="mb-3">In traditional Chinese medicine theory, nausea is understood as rebellious Qi — stomach energy flowing upward instead of downward. The Stomach's natural direction is described as downward; when disrupted by cold, dampness, heat, or emotional stress, Qi is said to reverse direction and produce the sensation of nausea. Acupressure points thought to restore downward Qi flow are therefore the ones traditionally favored. This is traditional theory, not a scientifically proven mechanism.</p></section><section id="pc6" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4"><a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">PC6 (Neiguan) — The Nausea Point</a></h2><p class="mb-4">No acupressure point on this site has more clinical study behind it than PC6 has for nausea. Known as Neiguan (Inner Gate), it sits on the inner forearm and has been examined in systematic reviews, most notably for post-operative nausea and vomiting.</p><div class="mb-6 p-4 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-900/20 border-l-4 border-amber-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm"><strong>Research note:</strong> A long-running Cochrane systematic review on P6/PC6 stimulation for post-operative nausea and vomiting (Lee A, Fan LTY, Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2009; CD003281, <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19370583/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300 dark:hover:text-forest-200">PMID 19370583</a>) found that non-invasive P6 acupressure reduces post-operative nausea and vomiting. The review's authors note that the overall quality of the underlying trials is mixed. This is the strongest single piece of evidence on this entire site.</p></div><p class="mb-2"><strong>Location:</strong> On the inner forearm, three finger-widths below the wrist crease, directly between the two prominent tendons. When you make a loose fist, the tendons become more visible. See our <a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:underline">detailed PC6 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-2"><strong>Why it works:</strong> In traditional Chinese medicine, PC6 belongs to the Pericardium meridian and is traditionally used to harmonize the Stomach and settle the upper digestive tract. Researchers are still investigating the biological mechanisms; hypotheses center on influences to the central nervous system pathways involved in nausea and on gut motility, but these mechanisms are not yet firmly established.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Turn your arm palm-up. Place your thumb directly between the two tendons at the marked location. Press firmly into the space — you should feel a distinct indentation. Hold for 2-3 minutes with steady pressure while breathing slowly. Repeat on the other arm. For continuous relief, acupressure wristbands (often sold as sea-sickness bands) can maintain pressure on PC6 for hours.</p></section><section id="other-points" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">Other Helpful Points for Nausea</h2><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">ST36 (Zusanli) — Leg Three Miles</h3><p class="mb-2"><strong>Location:</strong> Four finger-widths below the kneecap, one finger-width outward from the shinbone. See the <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:underline">ST36 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-2"><strong>Why it works:</strong> In traditional Chinese medicine, ST36 is traditionally known as a master point for digestive health. It is said to strengthen the Stomach and Spleen and regulate peristalsis, and it is traditionally favored when nausea comes with bloating, poor appetite, or general digestive weakness.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Use your thumb or knuckle to press firmly into the muscle just below the kneecap. Hold for 60-90 seconds per leg. You may feel a dull ache or sensation traveling down the leg toward the foot.</p><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">LI4 (Hegu) — Joining Valley</h3><p class="mb-2"><strong>Location:</strong> On the back of the hand in the web between thumb and index finger. Full <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:underline">LI4 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-2"><strong>Why it works:</strong> LI4 is a versatile point that is traditionally used for nausea, especially when it comes with headache or sinus congestion. In TCM terms it is said to regulate Qi flow through the upper body and head, which is also why it is a common headache point.</p><div class="mb-3 p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-900/20 border-l-4 border-amber-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm"><strong>Avoid during pregnancy.</strong> LI4 is traditionally considered contraindicated in pregnancy because it is thought to stimulate uterine contractions.</p></div><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">KD1 (Yongquan) — Gushing Spring</h3><p class="mb-2"><strong>Location:</strong> On the sole of the foot, in the depression that appears when the toes curl under, about one-third of the way from the toes toward the heel.</p><p class="mb-2"><strong>Why it works:</strong> KD1 is traditionally seen as a grounding point. In TCM it is said to draw energy downward, counteracting the upward rebellious Qi thought to cause nausea, and it is traditionally favored when nausea comes with dizziness, anxiety, or a floating sensation.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Sit comfortably and bring your foot onto your opposite thigh. Use your thumb to press firmly into the depression on the sole. Hold for 60 seconds per foot. This point can be surprisingly tender.</p></section><section id="how-to-apply" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">How to Apply Pressure for Nausea</h2><p class="mb-3">The technique for nausea differs slightly from other conditions. Because nausea often comes on suddenly and requires rapid relief, here is a modified approach:</p><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-3"><li>Use firmer, more sustained pressure — nausea often requires deeper stimulation than anxiety or sleep points.</li><li>Hold each point for 2-3 minutes minimum, rather than the usual 45-60 seconds.</li><li>Combine PC6 with deep diaphragmatic breathing. Inhale for 4 counts, hold for 4, exhale for 6.</li><li>For prevention, stimulate points 20-30 minutes before a known trigger (travel, chemotherapy session).</li><li>Acupressure wristbands are a practical option for maintaining continuous PC6 pressure during travel or long chemotherapy sessions.</li></ul></section><section id="by-cause" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Nausea by Cause: Tailored Approaches</h2><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Motion Sickness</h3><p class="mb-3">Stimulate PC6 continuously during travel. Acupressure wristbands are ideal here — they maintain constant pressure without requiring active effort. Combine with ST36 for best results. Apply pressure 20 minutes before departure for prevention.</p><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Morning Sickness (Pregnancy-Related Nausea)</h3><p class="mb-3">Gentle PC6 stimulation upon waking, before getting out of bed, is a popular approach for morning sickness, and the research here is generally positive though more uncertain than the post-operative evidence. LI4 is traditionally contraindicated during pregnancy. KD1 is a commonly suggested safe point that may help with the dizziness often accompanying first-trimester nausea. Always consult your obstetrician before using acupressure during pregnancy.</p><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Chemotherapy-Related Nausea</h3><p class="mb-3">Chemotherapy-induced nausea can be difficult to manage with medication alone. Research on acupressure for chemotherapy-related nausea is generally positive but mixed, and it is best used as a complement to your prescribed anti-emetic plan rather than in place of it. Apply pressure to PC6, ST36, and KD1 in sequence, spending 2-3 minutes on each point. Repeat the sequence every 2-4 hours during treatment cycles.</p></section><section id="evidence" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Scientific Evidence</h2><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-3"><li><strong>Post-operative Nausea:</strong> The Cochrane systematic review (Lee and Fan, 2009) found that non-invasive P6/PC6 stimulation reduces post-operative nausea and vomiting. This remains the strongest evidence on this site for any condition.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Evidence for acupressure on pregnancy-related nausea and vomiting is generally positive but more uncertain; reviews call for more and better-quality trials.</li><li><strong>Chemotherapy:</strong> Evidence for PC6 in chemotherapy-induced nausea is generally positive but mixed, and it is not a replacement for standard anti-emetic medication.</li><li><strong>Motion Sickness:</strong> Wristband-style PC6 stimulation is widely used for motion sickness, and some controlled studies have found a benefit, though results have been inconsistent.</li></ul></section><section id="faq" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="mb-6"><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">How quickly does acupressure work for nausea?</h3><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Many people feel some relief within 2-5 minutes of sustained PC6 pressure. If nausea does not improve after 10 minutes of continuous stimulation, repeat the sequence or try combining with other points like ST36.</p></div><div class="mb-6"><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">Can I use acupressure if I am already vomiting?</h3><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Yes. PC6 acupressure can be performed while actively feeling nauseous. If you cannot sit up, ask someone to apply pressure to your PC6 point. PC6 stimulation is also sometimes used in recovery-room care as a complement to anti-emetic medication.</p></div><div class="mb-6"><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">Do acupressure wristbands actually work?</h3><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Some clinical evidence supports their use, though results have been mixed. Wristbands with a hard plastic stud that presses directly into PC6 tend to work better than soft versions, and they are most useful for motion sickness and mild nausea rather than severe chemotherapy-induced nausea.</p></div><div class="mb-6"><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">Can acupressure replace anti-nausea medication?</h3><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">No, acupressure is a complementary therapy, not a replacement for prescribed medication. In studies it works best alongside standard anti-emetic drugs, enhancing their effectiveness rather than replacing them.</p></div><div class="mb-6"><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">When should I see a doctor for nausea?</h3><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Seek medical attention if nausea persists for more than 48 hours, is accompanied by severe abdominal pain, you cannot keep fluids down for more than 12 hours, or if you notice blood in your vomit. Nausea combined with severe headache or chest pain requires immediate evaluation.</p></div></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This content is for informational purposes only and is not medical advice. Consult a licensed healthcare provider before starting any new wellness practice.</p></div></article>
